WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.931
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.931 --> 00:00:02.527
Sunday the twenty-fifth.

00:00:02.527 --> 00:00:07.646
An easy one, with a civic errand up front and a dinner party to close it out.

00:00:07.646 --> 00:00:10.106
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:10.106 --> 00:00:16.689
With loudness normalization, what's the problem with the old way, just turning up the loudest peak?

00:00:16.689 --> 00:00:18.018
Answer's at the end.

00:00:18.018 --> 00:00:19.548
Weather's on your side.

00:00:19.548 --> 00:00:26.330
Sunny and dry all day, cool this morning and up to the mid-sixties by afternoon, with barely any wind.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:27.260
Okay, the day.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:28.524
Early voting first.

00:00:28.524 --> 00:00:31.383
You're at your polling site at ten to nine.

00:00:31.383 --> 00:00:34.308
Then nothing on your calendar until evening.

00:00:34.308 --> 00:00:40.758
On your radar, someone else is in a long class from one to seven, so the afternoon's yours alone.

00:00:40.758 --> 00:00:43.816
And you've got a dinner party at seven-thirty.

00:00:43.816 --> 00:00:44.747
Alright, news.

00:00:44.747 --> 00:00:45.744
Just two today.

00:00:45.744 --> 00:00:49.800
Canva put out its first image model of its own, called Craft.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.928
It's built a little differently.

00:00:51.928 --> 00:00:59.441
It plans the layout first, where things sit, how big they are, what the text says, and only then draws the image.

00:00:59.441 --> 00:01:07.686
It was trained on designed stuff like posters and menus instead of photos, so it's aimed at readable text and clean layouts.

00:01:07.686 --> 00:01:18.789
The part you'd care about is that the result can be split into editable layers, so you can grab the headline or move an object instead of regenerating the whole thing.

00:01:18.789 --> 00:01:22.313
It's in Canva's Dream Lab tab for everyone right now.

00:01:22.313 --> 00:01:28.496
And in France, another big Saturday of protests, with another march planned for next weekend.

00:01:28.496 --> 00:01:36.675
Official numbers put it around four hundred thousand people across the country, with unions saying it was closer to double.

00:01:36.675 --> 00:01:49.640
It started back in September with high school students blockading schools over crumbling buildings and teacher shortages, and it's grown into a wider fight over cost of living and the government.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.901
Okay, here's one for next weekend.

00:01:51.901 --> 00:01:58.749
Saturday is Halloween, and there are two free, low-key things close to home that aren't the big parade.

00:01:58.749 --> 00:02:09.786
Right on the edge of Bushwick, on Moore Street near the Moore Street Market, a community health group called Nos Cuidamos is hosting a Día de los Muertos celebration.

00:02:09.786 --> 00:02:12.512
It runs from two to seven, and it's free.

00:02:12.512 --> 00:02:17.765
It's the kind of thing that's more ofrenda and neighborhood than costume party.

00:02:17.765 --> 00:02:21.421
If you bring a camera, ask before you shoot the altars.

00:02:21.421 --> 00:02:28.336
Then later, over in Greenpoint, there's a haunted garden party in a community garden on Franklin Street.

00:02:28.336 --> 00:02:32.991
That's five to eight-thirty, also free, all ages, costumes encouraged.

00:02:32.991 --> 00:02:39.506
Easy loop: afternoon at the Día de los Muertos, then a slow ride up to Greenpoint as it gets dark.

00:02:39.506 --> 00:02:41.767
Today's challenge is a making one.

00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:44.560
Make something to bring to dinner tonight.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.887
Not a bottle from the corner store.

00:02:46.887 --> 00:02:49.347
Quick pickled red onions are perfect.

00:02:49.347 --> 00:02:57.458
Slice one onion thin, pack it in a jar, and pour over hot vinegar and water with a spoon of sugar and a big pinch of salt.

00:02:57.458 --> 00:03:01.714
Thirty minutes on the counter and they're bright pink and ready.

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:07.099
Showing up with a jar of something you made always lands better than you'd think.

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:09.559
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:09.559 --> 00:03:15.543
Remember wiring up the memo, where a push makes the audio and updates the feed on its own?

00:03:15.543 --> 00:03:18.668
Here's what's actually happening when you push.

00:03:18.668 --> 00:03:26.979
GitHub Actions is a set of instructions that lives in the repo, in a small file that says, when this happens, do these steps.

00:03:26.979 --> 00:03:28.708
The "when" is the trigger.

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:31.634
For the memo, the trigger is a push to main.

00:03:31.634 --> 00:03:36.487
When that push lands, GitHub spins up a fresh computer just for this job.

00:03:36.487 --> 00:03:37.219
It's blank.

00:03:37.219 --> 00:03:42.338
It clones the repo, installs whatever the steps need, and runs them in order.

00:03:42.338 --> 00:03:49.785
For the memo, that means reading the script, sending it to the voice, and turning the result into an audio file.

00:03:49.785 --> 00:03:52.179
Then that computer gets thrown away.

00:03:52.179 --> 00:04:01.354
Nothing on it survives, which is why every run starts from scratch and why anything you want to keep has to be saved somewhere on purpose.

00:04:01.354 --> 00:04:03.216
That's where Pages comes in.

00:04:03.216 --> 00:04:05.210
Pages is plain static hosting.

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:07.870
It doesn't run code when someone visits.

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:13.189
It just hands out files, like the audio and the feed, exactly as they were left.

00:04:13.189 --> 00:04:14.718
So the split is simple.

00:04:14.718 --> 00:04:17.444
Actions does the work once, at push time.

00:04:17.444 --> 00:04:21.833
Pages just serves the finished files, all day, to anyone who asks.

00:04:21.833 --> 00:04:26.886
So: vote at ten to nine, dinner party at seven-thirty, and bring the onions.

00:04:26.886 --> 00:04:32.670
The answer: one cough or loud plosive ends up setting the volume for the whole episode.

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:34.000
Enjoy the sun, Ziad.
